## ABSTRACT
Owing to unprecedented merits such as high theoretical capacity, superior energy density and low cost, lithium-sulfur batteries (LSBs) show a bright future both in scientific and industrial areas. Whereas, the inherent issues, including highly insulating character, undesired shuttle behavior and lithium dendrites growth, are seriously impeding its practical usage. Here, a metal-organic-frameworks (MOFs) derived N, S co-doped carbon nanotube hollow architecture confining with \(\mathsf{C o S}_{2}\) nanoparticles \(\mathsf{C o S}_{2}/\mathsf{N S C N H F}\) modified separator is designed to surmount these obstacles. Compared with Celgard separator, this designed separator shows obviously enhanced flame retardancy, giving \(73.1\%\) and \(53.0\%\) reductions in peak heat release rate and total heat release, separately. Concretely, its hollow structure, conductive feature, electrocatalytic activity and Lewis acid-base interaction enable the efficient inhibition on shuttle behavior as well as boost in polysulfides conversion kinetics. The cell with modified separator delivers a high discharge capacity of \(1,284.5\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\). After running for 100 cycles, a discharge capacity of \(661.3\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) is remained. Markedly, the suppression on lithium dendrites growth is also observed, manifesting the enhanced battery safety. Overall, this work may shed light on the effective usage of MOFs-derived hierarchical composite in achieving LSBs with high electrochemical performance as well as safety.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic-frameworks, shuttle behavior, lithium dendrites, flame retardancy
## 1 Introduction
Growing demand for electric vehicles and electronic products is the driving iterations of energy storage devices with high energy or power density and low cost [1, 2]. Lithium-sulfur batteries (LSBs) have been extensively investigated in both scientific and industrial fields as next-generation battery systems with promise for unparalleled theoretical capacity as well as energy density [3, 4]. Specifically, LSBs show a high theoretical capacity of \(1,675\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\), reflecting approximately tenfold capacity increase, in comparison to that of traditional lithium-ion batteries [5]. Notably, sulfur cathode materials used in LSBs possess the features of natural abundance, low cost and environmentally benignity. Despite these attractive merits, there are inherent and critical obstructions presented on the way of achieving the commercialization of LSBs [6, 7]. Firstly, sulfur and its solid discharge products (Li<sub>2</sub>S and Li<sub>2</sub>S<sub>2</sub>) are highly insulating and insoluble, resulting in the low utilization of active materials. Secondly, during cycling, highly-soluble lithium polysulfides (LiPSs, Li<sub>2</sub>S<sub>x</sub>, 4 ≤x ≤8) dissolve into the electrolyte and freely migrate to the lithium anode through the commercial separator. Subsequently, LiPSs are further reduced to Li<sub>2</sub>S and Li<sub>2</sub>S<sub>2</sub>. This action is defined as the "shuttle behavior", which eventually leads to irreversible capacity fading as well as low Coulombic efficiency (CE) of LSBs [8].
Tremendous efforts have been dedicated to address these above-mentioned issues by regulating of the structure and/or composition of cathode materials, including carbonaceous materials (porous hollow carbon [9], carbon nanotubes [10], graphene [11] and heteroatoms doped carbon [12]), conductive polymers (polyaniline [13], polypyrrole [14] and polythiophene [15]), and metal compounds (oxides [16], sulfides [17] and nitrides [18]). Nevertheless, excess passive weight is introduced into cathode, reducing the whole energy density. Designing three-dimensional (3D) binder-free cathode also stimulates the interest of researchers. However, the presence of 3D structure decreases the electrode tapping density and impairs the volumetric energy capacity [19]. Furthermore, the construction of cathode often involves with delicate and complicated procedures, which may substantially increase the fabricating cost and thus create extra hindrance to the practical usage of LSBs. The strategy of modifying separator with functional materials is promising in figuring out these issues of LSBs [20–23]. Carbonaceous materials have been extensively used to obtain modified separators. Manthiram et al. have prepared Super P carbon black coated separator for LSBs. After running at 0.2 C for 200 cycles, the cell delivered a discharge capacity of 828.0 mAh·g<sup>-1</sup> [24]. The potential of multi- and single-walled carbon nanotubes was also investigated by them [25, 26]. Unfortunately, the non-polar character results in its rather
weak interactions with polar LiPSs, causing the limited LiPSs trapping capability as well as insufficient inhibition on shuttle behavior. Utilizing of metal compounds as functional materials has also been proposed, owing to its high polarity and chemical adsorption ability to LiPSs. Zhang et al. have fabricated \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) coated Celgard separator for LSBs. After running for 50 cycles, this cell showed a reversible capacity of \(593.4~\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) [27]. \(\mathrm{MoO}_{3}\) decorated polypropylene separator was also prepared by Nahid Kaisar et al. [28]. However, metal oxides hold poor electrical conductivity, which slashes the electrode kinetics and thus compromises the reactivation of blocked LiPSs. In contrast, metal sulfides with high conductivity and strong chemical interaction for LiPSs are favored. Here, half-metallic \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) exhibits relatively high conductivity of \(6.7\times10^{3}\;\mathrm{S}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) at \(300~\mathrm{K}\), which far exceeds another transition metal sulfides such as \(\mathrm{FeS}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\), benefiting the formation of efficient electron pathways as well as improvement in electrocatalytic activity for polysulfide redox reactions [29]. Moreover, it is worth noting that integrating metal compounds with conductive substrates (such as graphene [30], heteroatoms doped carbon [31] and MXenes [32]) possesses satisfactory efficacy in accelerating the polysulfides conversion kinetics.
As with traditional LIBs, the severe growth of lithium dendrites deriving from uneven \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) plating/stripping is also existed in LSBs. Unfortunately, the parasitic reactions between LiPSs and lithium anode further aggravates the lithium dendrites growth [33, 34]. In consequence, the separator can be pierced by mossy and sharp lithium dendrites, causing internal short circuit and jeopardizing battery safety. Also, "dead lithium" species can be generated via the detachment of lithium dendrites and brittle electrolyte/electrode interface from lithium anode, reducing the lithium utilization. Notably, the connatural features of commercial separators, including low thermal stability and high flammability, are also detrimental to the safety and usage of LSBs [35, 36]. Therefore, designing flame retardant separators with the function of suppressing lithium dendrites growth is extremely urgent.
Profiting from ultra-high surface area, abundant porosity and multifaceted structure, metal-organic-frameworks (MOFs) have been under spotlight in past several years [37, 38]. By using MOFs as precursor, functional materials with specific structures can be obtained, which are in possession of superior potentials in energy storage area [39]. In this context, we have investigated the capability of N, S co-doped carbon nanotube hollow architecture confining with \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) nanoparticles \(\mathrm{(CoS_{2}/}\) NSCNHF) in LSBs, as functional materials for Celgard separator. The preparation of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}\) has been reported in our previous work [20]. Here, heteroatoms doping method is introduced to enhance the polarity of carbon structure, thus strengthening its chemical interactions with LiPSs. Compared with Celgard separator, the designed separator shows improved electrolyte affinity, thermal stability and flame retardancy. Also, the cell with modified separator shows markedly enhanced electrochemical properties than Celgard cell, which benefits from the superb shuttling behavior inhibition function of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}\). Surprisingly, the suppressed growth of lithium dendrites is also achieved, which is essential for improving the safety of battery.
## 2 Experimental
## 3 Results and discussion
The fabrication scheme of modified separator is given in Fig. 1. Obviously, the modified separator can be facilely obtained by vacuum filtration process. Morphologies of separators are investigated by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) technique, and the resultant images are shown in Fig. 2. Evidently, the nonpolar surface of Celgard separator shows consecutive pores with sizes up to several hundred nanometers, which is associated with the formation of severe shuttle behavior of LiPSs. Soluble LiPSs with size less than \(2~\mathrm{nm}\) can easily diffuse to the lithium anode, which triggers the parasitic reaction and thus deteriorates the cycling performance of LSBs. The surface morphology of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) is given in Fig. 2(b). It is apparent that, active \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}\) particles are homogenously distributed on the surface of Celgard separator. Firstly, this material holds N, S co-doped dodecahedron carbon structure, exhibiting favorable chemical adsorption ability to LiPSs and electrical conductivity for its redox [40, 41]. Abundant rooted CNTs can ulteriorly enhance the electrical conductivity of entire structure. Secondly, its rationally designed hollow structure with mesoporous feature as well as high specific surface area provides sufficient physical adsorption site for LiPSs. Thirdly, the generated polar \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) nanoparticles are regarded as efficient catalyst for boosting sulfur species conversion. Also, \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) provides additional chemical adsorption capacity to LiPSs, via the Lewis acid-base interaction. Overall, this modified layer is expected to be a promising dam for the flooded LiPSs, avoiding the loss of active sulfur materials and the corrosion of lithium anode. The thicknesses (\(\sim8.3\) and \(9.5\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\)) of coating layer are given in Figs. 2(c) and 2(d). Moreover, there is no obvious segregation between modified layer and Celgard separator, indicative of its good adhesion. As observed in SEM elemental mapping results (Fig. 2(e)), the S and Co signals are clearly presented in the upper of Celgard separator.
As is well known, the using of conventional Celgard separator is always obsessed by its low thermal stability and flame retardancy. Thus, the reinforcements in thermal property and fire safety are strongly desired, which can be achieved by utilizing modified layer. Thermogravimetry analysis (TGA) under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and micro-cone calorimeter (MCC) are employed to estimate the reinforcement effect. The corresponding results are given in Fig. S2 in the ESM [42-45]. When the temperature approaches to \(500\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), Celgard separator shows a lowest solid yield of \(4.43\;\mathrm{wt.}\%\). As for modified separator, the solid yields of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) and \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) separators are \(29.2\;\mathrm{wt.}\%\) and \(39.8\;\mathrm{wt.}\%\), respectively, demonstrating the improved thermal stability. Furthermore, Celgard separator gives a high maximum mass loss rate (MMLR) of \(2.632\;\%\cdot\mathrm{C}^{-1}\), whilst \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) and \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) separators exhibit the MMLR of 1.534 and \(1.311\;\%\cdot\mathrm{C}^{-1}\), associated with \(41.7\%\) and \(50.2\%\) reductions, separately. This implies the suppressed thermal decomposition [46, 47]. As given in Fig. 3, Celgard separator shows a sharp heat release curve with the peak heat release rate (PHRR) and total heat release (THR) values of \(1,342\;\mathrm{W\cdot g^{-1}}\) and \(71.7\;\mathrm{kJ\cdot g^{-1}}\), separately. In striking contrast, the \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) and \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) separators show the PHRR of 427 and \(361\;\mathrm{W\cdot g^{-1}}\), reflecting the marked reduction of \(68.2\%\) and \(73.1\%\), respectively. Furthermore, THR values are obviously decreased by \(44.8\%\) and \(53.0\%\), separately. These reductions evidence the promoted fire safety of separators [48-51]. The parameter of self-extinguishing time (SET) is also tested. Celgard separator shows a high SET value of \(3.03\;\mathrm{s\cdot mg^{-1}}\). As for the modified separator, the value sharply reduces to \(0.43\;\mathrm{s\cdot mg^{-1}}\), reflecting a decrease of \(85.8\%\), further corroborating the enhanced flame retardancy. The affinity of separator towards electrolyte is essential for the cell performance. Contact angle test is employed and the related results are portrayed in Fig. 3. Celgard separator shows an initial angle of \(45.4^{\circ}\). In comparison, the \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) and \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) separators exhibit original angles of \(16.0^{\circ}\) and \(17.9^{\circ}\), respectively. This obvious reduction on contact angle represents the improved electrolyte affinity,
which may contribute to \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transport and diffusion. After \(2\;\mathrm{s}\), Celgard separator remains a high angle of \(40.5^{\circ}\), while \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) and \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) separators give low angle of \(9.0^{\circ}\) and \(0^{\circ}\), separately. This reflects the better infiltration of electrolyte towards modified separator. We also evaluate the corresponding electrolyte uptake of these separators. Clearly, Celgard separator gives an uptake of \(83.71\%\), whilst the modified separators present uptakes of \(126.2\%\) and \(168.0\%\). The abundant and relatively-high specific surface area characters of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}\), as reported previously, are responsible for the electrolyte uptake increase. Ionic conductivities of these separators are calculated by electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) plots. Celgard separator shows a conductivity of \(0.467\;\mathrm{mS\cdot cm^{-1}}\), while the modified separators perform enhanced conductivities of \(0.777\) and \(0.669\;\mathrm{mS\cdot cm^{-1}}\), directly proving the positive effect of modified layers on \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transport. Mechanical performance of separator is also of significance for its practical usage. Figure 3 also gives the digital pictures of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) separator after folding and bending treatments. There are no detachments observed for modified layer, confirming its good mechanical property.
The facile adsorption test of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}\) towards LiPSs is performed and the corresponding results are shown in Fig. 4. The color of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) solution with \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}\) powders changes from brownish to approximately colorless, after shaking for \(3\;\mathrm{s}\). This directly confirms the adsorption effect of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}\). The marked decreased polysulfides absorbance in UV-Vis spectra also verifies adsorption effect of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}\). After placing for \(12\;\mathrm{h}\), the \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}\) powders are collected for subsequent XPS experiment. In Li 1s spectrum, the peaks at 55.8 and \(57.0\;\mathrm{eV}\) are corresponding to Li-S and Li-N bonds, separately [52]. Li-S bond in \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) situates at \(56.3\;\mathrm{eV}\). The position decrease of \(0.5\;\mathrm{eV}\) indicates the strong interaction between \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) and \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}\), resulting in the electron transfer. The presence of Li-N bond confirms the affinity of doped N atoms towards \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) molecules. Co 2p spectrum is also exhibited, and two peaks at 781.3 and \(798.1\;\mathrm{eV}\) corresponds to Co 2p 3/2 and Co 2p 1/2, respectively. Compared with the
result of original \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}\), the peaks are reduced by 0.1 and \(0.2\,\mathrm{eV}\). The position decrease phenomenon is also observed for satellite peaks. In N 1s spectrum, the peak at \(403.0\;\mathrm{eV}\) can be attributed to N-Li bond, directly proving the interaction between doped N atoms and \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) molecules [53]. Three peaks at 401.7, 400.3 and \(398.8~\mathrm{eV}\) belong to graphitic N, pyrrolic N and pyridinic N, separately [54–57]. In S 2p spectrum, the peaks at 170.3 and \(169.1~\mathrm{eV}\) are assigned to polythionates, and the signal at \(167.9~\mathrm{eV}\) is ascribed to thiosulfates [58]. Additionally, the signals at 165.0 and \(163.9~\mathrm{eV}\) correspond to C-S-C bond and \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\), respectively. Meanwhile, the peaks at 163.1 and \(161.9\,\mathrm{eV}\) are attributed to bridging S atoms \((S_{\mathrm{B}}^{\mathrm{B}})\) and terminal S atoms \((S_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{B}-1})\) [59, 60]. The emerging of the \(S_{\mathrm{B}}^{\mathrm{B}}\) means that the majority of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) molecules are strongly anchored on the \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}\) surface.
As mentioned above, the intermediate products of LiPSs play a vital role in affecting the electrochemical performance of LSBs. Thus, it is necessary to evaluate the diffusion character of LiPSs. Whereas, the diffusion coefficient of LiPSs is hard to be acquired directly through electrochemical measurement. Fortunately, the diffusivity of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) can be figured out, which is capable of revealing the surface diffusion of LiPSs. High LiPSs surface diffusion facilitates its electrochemical reaction and controllable deposition, retaining the sulfur species active and rendering the superior cycling property of cells. Thus, the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) diffusion coefficient \((D_{\mathrm{Li}}^{+})\) can be deemed as valid indicator for the chemical conversion process of sulfur. On the basis of cyclic voltammetry (CV) plots at various scan rates (as plotted in Fig. 5), the \(D_{\mathrm{Li}}{}^{+}\) values can be reckoned by the Randles-Sevick equation. Two representative cathodic peaks located at 2.31 and \(2.02\,\mathrm{~V~}\) can be ascribed to the transformation of sulfur to soluble LiPSs and soluble LiPSs to solid-state \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S},\) respectively. Two anodic signals are derived from the conversion of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) to LiPSs as well as sulfur. The mentioned cathodic peaks are defined as peaks A and B, and the anodic peak at \(\sim2.4\:\mathrm{V}\) is determined as peak C. For Celgard cell, \(D_{\mathrm{Li}^{+}}\) (A), \(D_{\mathrm{Li}^{+}}\) (B) and \(D_{\mathrm{Li}^{+}}\) (C) are calculated to be \(3.43\times10^{-9}\) , \(6.64\times10^{-9}\) and \(1.18\,\times\,10^{-8}\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) , separately. For \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}@C-100\) cell, \(D_{\mathrm{Li}^{+}}\) (A), \(D_{\mathrm{Li}^{+}}\) (B) and \(D_{\mathrm{Li}^{+}}\) (C) are calculated to be \(6.59\times10^{-9}\) , \(1.15\,\times\,10^{-8}\) and \(4.38\,\times\,10^{-8}\,\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) , separately. For \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\) \(\mathrm{NSCNHF}@C{-}200\) cell, \(D_{\mathrm{Li}^{+}}\) (A), \(D_{\mathrm{Li}^{+}}\) (B) and \(D_{\mathrm{Li}^{+}}\) (C) are calculated to be \(7.64\times10^{-9}\) , \(1.35\times10^{-8}\) and \(2.91\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) , separately. The markedly enhanced \(D_{\mathrm{Li}^{+}}\) proves that using modified separator indeed imparts the cell with reduced ion diffusion impedance and promoted redox transformation of sulfur species. Evidently, the cells with modified separators show significantly increased redox peak intensity, corroborating the importance of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}\) in accelerating the electrochemical activity of LiPSs. Furthermore, CV curves of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}@C-100\) cell show positive and negative shifts in reduction and oxidation peaks, respectively, at various scan rates (as shown in Fig. S3 in the ESM). This ulteriorly manifests the rapid polysulfide redox kinetics. CV plots at \(6.2\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) of different cells are given in Fig. S4 in the ESM. Obviously, \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}@C-100\) cell shows higher response current and better repeatability than Celgard cell, which indicates that using modified separator endows battery with improved cycling stability. Potential gap \((\Delta E)\) between reductive and oxidative peaks is also reckoned. Celgard cell shows \(\Delta E\) values of 0.299, 0.284, 0.268 and \(0.257\mathrm{~V~}\) at \(2^{\mathrm{nd}},\,3^{\mathrm{rd}},\,4^{\mathrm{th}}\) and \(5^{\mathrm{th}}\) cycle, separately. \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}@C-100\) cell gives \(\Delta E\) values of 0.051, 0.042, 0.037 and \(0.037\mathrm{~V~}\) at \(2^{\mathrm{nd}},\,3^{\mathrm{rd}},\,4^{\mathrm{th}}\) and \(5^{\mathrm{th}}\) cycle, respectively. The obvious reductions in \(\Delta E\) suggest the enhanced reaction kinetics.
