\(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) cell shows reduced polarization voltage, compared with Celgard cell. The corresponding data of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{C}\) counterpart is presented in Fig. S6 in the ESM. The discharge capacities at \(1^{\mathrm{st}}\), \(10^{\mathrm{th}}\), \(30^{\mathrm{th}}\), \(50^{\mathrm{th}}\) and \(100^{\mathrm{th}}\) are 510.2, 446.2, 390.0, 353.7 and \(291.6\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\), respectively. This poor cycling performance can be attributed to the lack of conductive carbon structure. In the first charge-discharge curves, the upper discharge plateau (\(\sim2.3\mathrm{~V~}\)) corresponds to the conversion
390.0, 353.7 and \(291.6\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) , respectively. This poor cycling performance can be attributed to the lack of conductive carbon structure. In the first charge-discharge curves, the upper discharge plateau \(\mathrm{~\sim~}2.3\mathrm{~V~}\) ) corresponds to the conversion
of sulfur to soluble LiPSs, while the lower discharge plateau \((\sim\,2.10\,\mathrm{~V~})\) indicates the transformation of soluble LiPSs to \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) [61]. This is in accordance with CV results. Notably, the \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}@C-200\) cell exhibits a duplication of charge platforms, instead of the obvious segregation, as shown for Celgard cell. This illustrates the enhanced electrochemical stability. The capacities at higher and lower discharge platforms ( \(Q_{\mathrm{H}}\) vs. \(Q_{\mathrm{L}}\) ) can be acquired from these profiles, and the corresponding data are given in Fig. 7. Here, \(Q_{\mathrm{H}}\) is derived from the generation of soluble LiPSs, which causes the shuttle effect in LSBs, while \(Q_{\mathrm{L}}\) is assigned to the generation of solid \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\), incurring depressed reaction kinetics. Explicitly, \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}@C-200\) cell delivers a high incipient \(Q_{\mathrm{H}}\) of \(379.5\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) and capacity retention of \(56.5\%\) after 100 cycles. This is superior than \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}@C-100\) cell with an incipient \(Q_{\mathrm{H}}\) of \(364.7\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) \((51.9\%)\) or Celgard cell with an inceptive \(Q_{\mathrm{H}}\) of \(255.0\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) \((37.9\%)\). These results imply that the shuttling of LiPSs is greatly suppressed by \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}\) layer. Likewise, \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}@C-200\) cell gives a high incipient \(Q_{\mathrm{L}}\) of \(907.3\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) and capacity retention of \(49.2\%\) after 100 cycles. As for Celgard cell, the corresponding data are \(602.4\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) and \(28.2\%\). Generally, the \(Q_{\mathrm{L}}\) loss tendency of cells with modified separators is much slower, associated with the high activity and rapid redox reaction of the \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\). The value of \(Q_{\mathrm{L}}/Q_{\mathrm{H}}\) is used to reflect the catalytic ability for LiPSs reduction reaction. \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}@C-200\) cell shows a \(Q_{\mathrm{L}}/Q_{\mathrm{H}}\) value of 2.08 at \(100^{\mathrm{th}}\) cycle, higher than that of Celgard cell (1.75), which is due to the catalytic function of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\). Since \(Q_{\mathrm{L}}\) is three times of \(Q_{\mathrm{L}}\) in theory (1256 vs. \(419\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\)), the value of \(Q_{\mathrm{L}}/3Q_{\mathrm{H}}\) is regarded as indicator of soluble S utilization [62]. As shown in Fig. 7(f), Celgard cell shows soluble S utilization of \(78.7\%\) and \(58.5\%\) at \(1^{\mathrm{st}}\) and \(100^{\mathrm{th}}\) cycle. Excitingly, the soluble S utilization of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}@C-200\) cell obviously elevates to \(79.7\%\) and \(69.4\%\) at \(1^{\mathrm{st}}\) and \(100^{\mathrm{th}}\) cycle, respectively, demonstrating the efficient impedance of shuttle behavior and thus reutilization of LiPSs.
Rate performance of cells is also estimated to confirm the superiority of modified separators. Celgard cell gives an initial discharge capacity of \(315.2\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\), reflecting a capacity
utilization of \(48.7\%\) (considering the theoretical capacity of \(1,675\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\)). When the current density is elevated to 0.2, 0.5, 1 and \(2\;\mathrm{C}\), the discharge capacities are 565, 425.6, 348.3 and \(244.4\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\), respectively. The low discharge capacity at \(2\;\mathrm{C}\) indicates the sluggish kinetics of LiPSs redox reactions. By contrast, \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}@C-100\) cell presents an inceptive discharge capacity of \(1,189.5\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\), showing a capacity utilization of \(71.0\%\), much higher than that of Celgard cell. Moreover, the discharge capacities at 0.2, 0.5, 1 and \(2\;\mathrm{C}\) are 1,098.0, 872.2, 709.3 and \(522.0\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\), separately. The discharge capacity at \(2\;\mathrm{C}\) is improved by \(113.6\%\), in comparison to that of Celgard cell. \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}@C-200\) cell delivers the further improved discharge capacities of 1,283.1, 1,106.8, 901.7, 756.3 and \(532.1\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\), separately. High capacities of 980.6 and \(1,015.3\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) can be resumed after abruptly switching the current density back to \(0.1\;\mathrm{C}\), while a low capacity of \(385.1\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) is recovered for Celgard cell. The related capacity retention (considering their initial capacities) of Celgard cell is \(47.2\%\). After using modified separators, the values are elevated to \(82.4\%\) and \(79.0\%\), highlighting the improved stability of cells at various current rates. The discharge-charge profiles at different current rate of these cells are also depicted. The corresponding discharge/charge plateaus are clearly observed and in line with CV results. The resultant \(Q_{\mathrm{H}}\) and \(Q_{\mathrm{L}}\) data are summarized at Fig. 8. \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}@C-200\) cell shows a high original \(Q_{\mathrm{H}}\) of \(331.0\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) and a capacity retention of \(75.2\%\) at \(2\;\mathrm{C}\). However, Celgard cell only shows a low inceptive \(Q_{\mathrm{H}}\) of \(159.5\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) and a capacity retention of \(70.8\%\) at \(2\;\mathrm{C}\). This promotion is attributed to the suppression effect on LiPSs shuttling, arising from modified layer. Furthermore, \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}@C-200\) cell presents a high incipient \(Q_{\mathrm{L}}\) of \(953.0\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) and capacity retention of \(30.0\%\) at \(2\;\mathrm{C}\). As for Celgard cell, the corresponding data are \(657.0\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) and \(20.2\%\). This demonstrates that using modified separator facilitates the redox reaction of the \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\). These results together manifest that, the cell with modified separator displays promoted rate property. At \(0.5\;\mathrm{C}\), Celgard cell shows \(Q_{\mathrm{L}}/Q_{\mathrm{H}}\) and \(Q_{\mathrm{L}}/3Q_{\mathrm{H}}\) values of 2.127 and \(70.9\%\), which are increased to 2.197 and \(73.2\%\), by utilizing \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}@C-200\) separator.
In order to figure out the positive role of modified layer during the long cycling stability comprehensively, the electrochemical performance of cells using electrolyte without \(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}\) additive is also investigated. The corresponding plots are shown in Fig. S7 in the ESM. Celgard cell gives an initial discharge capacity of \(1,072.4\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) at \(0.1\;\mathrm{C}\). When suddenly shifting the current rate to \(1\;\mathrm{C}\), a low discharge capacity of \(434.1\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) can be
found. As for \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) cell, the inceptive discharge capacities at 0.1 and \(1\,\,\,C\) are 1,299.3 and \(564.0\,\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) , respectively. \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cell shows more favorable cycling performance, giving the capacities of 1,410.5 and \(829.3\,\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) at 0.1 and \(1\,\,\,C,\) separately. Compared with Celgard cell, the increases are reckoned to be \(31.5\%\) and \(91.0\%\) , respectively. In addition, the discharge capacities at \(100^{\mathrm{th}}\) cycle are \(368.7\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) for Celgard cell, \(550.9\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) for \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) cell and \(660.3~\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) for \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cell. This capacity promotion strongly confirms the superb capability of modified layer for suppressing the shuttle effect of LiPSs.
We also investigate the electrochemical property of cells with higher sulfur loading \((\sim2.04\mathrm{~mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) , and the related plots are given in Fig. S8 in the ESM. Celgard cell shows an initial charge capacity of \(427.9\;\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) with a capacity retention of \(25.5\%\) , while \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cell gives an inceptive charge capacity of \(1,408.6\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) with a capacity retention of \(84.1\%\) . When turning the current density to \(0.5\,\mathrm{C},\) the charge capacities of Celgard and \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cells are 500.2 and \(691.8\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) , respectively. These improvements demonstrate the superiority of modified separator in suppressing the shuttle behavior of LiPSs, even under high sulfur loading. After 100 cycles, \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cell gives a high charge capacity of \(740.9\,\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) . In sharp contrast, Celgard cell shows an inferior cycling performance with a low charge capacity of \(387.7\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) . Moreover, there are capacity increase presented in the cycling process for these two cells, which is common phenomenon in LSBs with high sulfur loading. This can be attributed to the rearrangement of the active material as it conditions itself to occupy the more electrochemically favorable positions. The detailed charge profiles at different cycles are given in Fig. S8(d) in the ESM. Celgard cell shows the charge capacities at \(2^{\mathrm{nd}}\) , \(10^{\mathrm{th}}\) , \(30^{\mathrm{th}}\) , \(50^{\mathrm{th}}\)
and \(80^{\mathrm{th}}\) are 480.3, 483.9, 577.3, 511.9 and \(431.6\,\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) , separately. By contrast, \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cell shows the charge capacities at \(2^{\mathrm{nd}}\) , \(10^{\mathrm{th}}\) , \(30^{\mathrm{th}}\) , \(50^{\mathrm{th}}\) and \(80^{\mathrm{th}}\) are 963.2, 717.2, 807.2, 803.2 and \(771.4\,\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) , separately. Moreover, the well overlapped charge platforms can be observed for the \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cell, implying its high electrochemical stability. Rate property of these cells is also evaluated, and the results are given in Fig. S8(b) in the ESM. \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-}\) 200 cell gives the discharge capacities of 540.4, 621.1, 487.1 and \(375.0\,\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) at 0.1, 0.5, 1 and \(2\,C,\) respectively. It's worth noting that a high discharge capacity of \(1,114.7\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) is obtained after running at \(0.1\,\mathrm{~C~}\) for 5 cycles. As for Celgard cell, the corresponding capacities at 0.1, 0.5, 1 and \(2\,\mathrm{C}\) are 590.1, 500.2, 430.7 and \(324.7\,\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) , separately. The discharge-charge plots of cells are clearly exhibited in Figs. S6(d) and S6(e) in the ESM.
As is well known, LSBs are suffered from its self-discharge behavior, which causes the serious capacity decay and thus restrains its commercialization. The suppression effects of modified separators on the self-discharge behavior of cells are deeply investigated. Figures 9(a)–9(c) show the discharge-charge profiles of these cells under normal condition at \(0.2~C\) . As for Celgard cell, the discharge capacities at \(1^{\mathrm{st}}\) , \(2^{\mathrm{nd}}\) , \(3^{\mathrm{rd}}\) and \(4^{\mathrm{th}}\) are 904.8, 864.5, 801.0 and \(769.9\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) , respectively. Here, the capacity retention is determined as the capacity ratio of the former and latter cycle. Then, the capacity retention of Celgard cell at \(2^{\mathrm{nd}}\) , \(3^{\mathrm{rd}}\) and \(4^{\mathrm{th}}\) are \(95.6\%\) , \(88.4\%\) and \(84.9\%\) , respectively. As for \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) cell, the discharge capacities are 1,285.2, 1,259.3, 1,210.3 and \(1,176.3\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) , and the related capacity retention values are \(97.8\%\) , \(94.1\%\) , \(91.5\%\) . Likewise, with regard to \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cell, the discharge capacities are 1,307.2, 1,273.1, 1,219.2 and \(1,193.6\,\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) , and the pertinent capacity retention values are \(97.4\%\) , \(93.3\%\) and \(91.3\%\) . Figures 9(e)–9(g) present the discharge–charge profiles of these cells under particular condition (the cell is rested for
24 h after one discharge-charge cycle) at 0.2 C. Celgard cell gives the discharge capacities of 962.8, 622.9, 518.6 and 454.4 mAh·g\(^{-1}\) at \(1^{\mathrm{st}}\), \(2^{\mathrm{nd}}\), \(3^{\mathrm{rd}}\) and \(4^{\mathrm{th}}\), respectively. The capacity retention values are 64.8%, 53.6% and 47.2%. Then, the difference between capacity retention under normal and particular condition (\(\Delta R\) values) are 30.8%, 34.8% and 37.7%. Similarly, the \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) cell shows the discharge capacities of 1,356.5, 1,167.9, 1,117.1 and 1,032.5 mAh·g\(^{-1}\), giving the capacity retention values of 86.2%, 82.7% and 76.2%. Consequently, the \(\Delta R\) values are 11.6%, 11.4% and 15.3%. As for \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cell, the capacity retention values are 92.1%, 87.9% and 74.1%, and the \(\Delta R\) values are calculated to be 5.3%, 5.4% and 17.2%. Generally, the cells with modified separators exhibit much lower capacity loss than the cell using Celgard separator, when 24 h resting process is employed. This corroborates the suppression on self-discharge behavior, deriving from the modified layer with abundant physical and chemical interaction sites with LiPSs. Self-discharge behavior is also investigated by monitoring the open circuit voltage (OCV) change during the statically placing process. The OCV retention (calculated by the ratio of current voltage to original voltage) plots for these cells are given Fig. 9(h). After 200 h storing, the retention ratio of Celgard cell decreases to 92.8%, confirming its severe self-discharge. Strikingly, the retention ratio of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cell remains at 100.4%, demonstrating the superiority of modified layer on self-discharge behavior.
We also evaluate the cycling performance of cells after statically placing for 7 d. As given in Fig. S9 in the ESM, Celgard cell shows an initial discharge capacity of 565.3 mAh·g\(^{-1}\). After 50 cycles, a discharge capacity of 301.8 mAh·g\(^{-1}\) is delivered. On the contrary, the \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) cell gives an incentive discharge capacity of 870.0 mAh·g\(^{-1}\), and a discharge capacity of 508.3 mAh·g\(^{-1}\) can be remained. Compared with Celgard cell, the capacity increases are 53.9% and 68.5%, respectively. The corresponding discharge-charge plots at different cycles are given in Figs. S9(b)–S9(f) in the ESM. For Celgard cell, the discharge capacities at 5\(^{\mathrm{th}}\), 10\(^{\mathrm{th}}\) and 30\(^{\mathrm{th}}\) are 484.3, 389.7 and 343.2 mAh·g\(^{-1}\), respectively. For \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) cell, the discharge capacities at 5\(^{\mathrm{th}}\), 10\(^{\mathrm{th}}\) and 30\(^{\mathrm{th}}\) are 836.7, 631.7 and 556.4 mAh·g\(^{-1}\), separately. These results clearly evidence that, the modified separator possesses effective inhibition function on self-discharge behavior of LSBs cells. Also, the \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) cell shows higher discharge platform as well as lower charge platform than Celgard cell, corroborating the improved reaction kinetics.
EIS analysis is employed to investigate the electrochemical kinetics before and after cycling. For freshly prepared cells, the Nyquist plots are composed of a medium-to-high frequency semicircle and a long-inclined line (Warburg impedance) in the low frequency region. The equivalent circuit models for analyzing the impedance spectra are shown in Figs. S10(c) and S10(f) in the ESM. The high-frequency intercept on the real axis represents the ohmic resistance (\(R_{\mathrm{c}}\)), containing the electrolyte and electrode resistances. The semicircle at medium-to-high frequency corresponds to the interface charge-transfer resistance (\(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\)), and the inclined line at the low-frequency region indicates the Warburg impedance (\(W_{1}\)), which is associated with the semi-infinite diffusion of soluble LiPSs in the electrolyte. Also, CPE is the corresponding constant phase element about the double layer capacitance [63, 64]. Celgard cell gives the \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) of 50.00 \(\Omega\). As for \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) and \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cells, the \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) values are 11.55 and 11.38 \(\Omega\), separately. The remarkable decreases in \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) for cells with modified separators can be attributed to the high electrical conductivity of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}\) functional material, which
can serve as a second current collector, lowering the internal resistance of cells. Owing to the presence of self-discharge process during the 7 d placing process, a value of \(R_{\mathrm{f}}\) representing the deposit diffusion resistance of the solid electrolyte interphase (SEI) film, can be figured out (as given in Fig. S10 in the ESM). This resistance is contributed by the passive \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) layer on the surface of electrode. Celgard cell shows the \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) and \(R_{\mathrm{f}}\) of 14.67 and 46.10 \(\Omega\), respectively. As for \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) cell, the \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) and \(R_{\mathrm{f}}\) of 3.757 and 17.50 \(\Omega\), respectively. With regard to \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cell, the corresponding values are 3.350 and 7.040 \(\Omega\), separately. Interestingly, all cells exhibit lower \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) after cycling process, which may be caused by the sufficient infiltration of electrolyte. Compared with Celgard cell, the cells with modified separators show obviously reduced \(R_{\mathrm{f}}\), implying that modified layer can effectively adsorb the formed LiPSs, thus restraining the formation of passive \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) layer on lithium anode.
EIS spectra of these cells after cycling and rating tests are given in Fig. 10 and Fig. S11 in the ESM, and the related resistance values are also presented. For Celgard cell, the \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) values at \(10^{\mathrm{th}}\), \(30^{\mathrm{th}}\), \(50^{\mathrm{th}}\) and \(80^{\mathrm{th}}\) are 19.12, 28.91, 29.44 and 30.65 \(\Omega\), separately. Meanwhile, the \(R_{\mathrm{f}}\) values are 21.49, 18.46, 16.02 and 15.87 \(\Omega\), separately. By using \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) separator, the \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) values at \(10^{\mathrm{th}}\), \(30^{\mathrm{th}}\), \(50^{\mathrm{th}}\) and \(80^{\mathrm{th}}\) are 1.509, 2.029, 2.442 and 2.010 \(\Omega\), respectively, showing the decreases of 92.1%, 93.0%, 91.7% and 93.4%. Also, the \(R_{\mathrm{f}}\) values are 5.445, 4.213, 6.659 and 7.902 \(\Omega\), respectively, reflecting the reductions of 74.7%, 77.2%, 58.4% and 50.2%. These dramatically decreases in resistance values strongly confirm the meliority of modified layer in promoting the redox kinetics. Firstly, the N, S co-doped dodecahedron carbon structure with rooted CNTs possesses high electrical conductivity and thus acts as pseudo-current collector, lowering the \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) of cells [65]. Secondly, its hollow structure with mesoporous feature and high specific surface area facilitates the physical adsorption to LiPSs [66]. Thirdly, the polar \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) nanoparticles hold the ability of catalyzing the sulfur species conversion. Additionally, the interaction between \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) and LiPSs by forming Co-S bond
contributes to the chemical adsorption of LiPSs [67]. Then, the shuttling behavior of LiPSs from cathode to anode is suppressed, and the growth of passive \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) layer can be greatly inhibited, leading to reduced \(R_{\mathrm{f}}\) values. Likewise, \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cell presents the \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) values at \(10^{\mathrm{th}}\), \(30^{\mathrm{th}}\), \(50^{\mathrm{th}}\) and \(80^{\mathrm{th}}\) are 1.334, 6.633, 1.804 and \(8.261\ \Omega\), separately, corresponding to the decreases of \(93.0\%\) , \(77.1\%\) , \(93.9\%\) and \(73.0\%\) . In addition, the \(R_{\mathrm{f}}\) values are 4.257, 2.991, 7.090 and \(6.628\ \Omega\) , respectively, reflecting the reductions of \(80.2\%\) , \(83.8\%\) , \(55.7\%\) and \(58.2\%\) . Compared with their fresh state, all cells show decreased \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) , which can be attributed to the permeation of the electrolyte and chemical activation process for the dissolution as well as redistribution of the active materials. Furthermore, the EIS spectra of these cells after rate test are analyzed in detail. As for Celgard cell, the \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) and \(R_{\mathrm{f}}\) values are 28.74 and \(43.66\ \Omega\) , respectively. By contrast, the \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) cell gives the lower \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) and \(R_{\mathrm{f}}\) values of 8.341 and \(9.947\,\Omega\) , respectively. The reductions are reckoned as \(71.0\%\) and \(77.2\%\) , separately. With regard to \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cell, the \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) and \(R_{\mathrm{f}}\) values are 9.940 and \(9.583\;\Omega\) , corresponding to the decreases of \(65.4\%\) and \(78.1\%\) , separately. These declines in \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) and \(R_{\mathrm{f}}\) firmly verify that, using modified separators boosts the fast \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transfer and efficient LiPSs capturing as well as converting. Benefiting from this, the improved cycling and rating performances can be achieved, as mentioned above.
For the sake of acquiring a comprehensive understanding of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) diffusion performance, \(D_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}\) can be reckoned according to the follow equations
\[
\begin{array}{rl}&{\omega=2\pi f}\\&{Z^{\prime}=R_{\mathrm{s}}+R_{\mathrm{ct}}+R_{\mathrm{f}}+\sigma\omega^{-1/2}}\\&{D=0.5R^{2}T^{2}/S^{4}n^{4}F^{4}C^{2}\sigma^{2}}\end{array}
\]
Here, \(R,\ T,\ S\) and \(n\) represent the gas constant, absolute temperature, surface area of the electrode, number of electrons per molecule during oxidization, respectively. \(F\) and \(C\) are the Faraday constant and concentration of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) . \(\sigma\) signifies the Warburg factor, which can be gained from the linear fitting plots between \(Z^{\prime}\) and \(\omega^{-1/2}\) in the low frequency [68]. As shown in Fig. 11, fresh Celgard cell shows a \(D_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}\) of \(2.2\times10^{-11}\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}.\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) . After using modified separators, the \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) and \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cells exhibit \(D_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}\) values of \(8.7\times10^{-11}\) and \(2.3\times10^{-10}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}.\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) , respectively. The \(D_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}\) values after 30
cycles are also calculated as \(3.8\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}.\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) for Celgard cell, \(1.6\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}.\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) for \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) cell and \(2.4\times10^{-10}\) \(\mathrm{cm}^{2}.\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) for \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cell. The improvements in \(D_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}\) values declare the efficacy of modified layer in ameliorating \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) diffusion. Furthermore, the \(D_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}\) values after statically placing and rating tests are figured out. Taking the values after rating test as an instance, Celgard cell shows a \(D_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}\) of \(1.1\times10^{-11}\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}.\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) , whilst the \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-100}\) and \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cells exhibit \(D_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}\) values of \(6.4\times10^{-11}\) and \(3.2\times10^{-11}\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}.\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) , separately. These results further affirm the positive effect of modified layer on \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) diffusion and consequent enhanced electrochemical properties.
In order to obtain clarity to the function of the modified separator, LSBs after cycling and rating tests are disassembled and exquisitely investigated by postmortem analysis. Figure S12 in the ESM gives the SEM morphologies of Celgard separator and modified separators against lithium anode. Clearly, Celgard separator possesses rough surface and severely blocked hole structure, resulting from the dense aggregation of solid LiPSs. During the cycling, Celgard separator shows no suppression effect on the shuttling behavior of LiPSs. Then, the permeated LiPSs are easily deposited on the surface of Celgard separator. Due to the scant accessibility to conductive matrix, these deposited sulfur species are regarded as "dead sulfur" and rarely participate in the redox reaction, which ultimately induces the loss of active materials. Also, these sediments impede the pathways for \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) diffusion, which is detrimental to the cycling performance and rate capability of cells. In sharp contrast, the \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) separator shows relatively smooth surface with evident hole structure, ensuring the fluency of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) diffusion. Notably, after rating test, no obvious LiPSs can be observed on the surface of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) separator, while severe aggregations are presented in the holes of Celgard separator. This demonstrates the excellent polysulfides-retarding ability of modified layer, deriving from the capturing and catalyzing effect of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}\) . Also, we investigate the SEM morphologies of these separators against cathode (as shown in Fig. S13 in the ESM). Both after cycling and rating tests, Celgard separator retains a smooth surface and the abundant hole structure can be clearly observed, which is contrary to the result of Celgard separator against lithium anode. This phenomenon exactly illustrates the inferior polysulfides-retarding ability of Celgard separator. The soluble LiPSs can
easily permeate Celgard separator and consequently accumulate on the surface, dampening the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transferring. Also, the side reaction between the penetrative LiPSs and active lithium anode deteriorates the growth of lithium dendrites, which is considered as major threat to the battery safety. As for \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) separator, there is a marked change in morphology after test. The modified layer is fully filled with generated sulfur species, which is different with its counterpart, demonstrating the efficient capturing of LiPSs. This derives from the cooperative effect of physical barrier and chemical affinity, rendered by heteroatoms doping, hollow structure as well as polar \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) nanoparticles. The blocked sulfur species can be reactivated and reutilized, participating in the redox reaction and thus avoiding the serious active materials loss. The above results jointly corroborate that the modified layer has the efficacy in inhibiting the shuttle behavior of LiPSs and preserving them on the cathode side. In consequence, the promoted sulfur utilization and subsequent enhanced cycling as well as rate properties can be achieved. SEM morphologies of lithium anode after cycling and rate testes are also presented in Fig. 12. Clearly, there are many cracks and protrusions on the anode surface of Celgard cell, attributed to the severe corrosion action of LiPSs. Meanwhile, the corrosion action can cause the rupture of protective SEI film, which consequently induces the deteriorative growth of lithium dendrites. On the contrary, a relatively-smooth surface is found for \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cell, reflecting delayed corrosion action. This can be assigned to the superb inhibiting effect of modified separator on LiPSs shuttling. Elemental mapping results of lithium anodes are given in Fig. S14 in the \(\mathrm{ESM}\). Obviously, the intensity of S signal in lithium anode of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cell is much weaker than that of Celgard cell, directly confirming the polysulfides-inhibiting function of modified layer. Inhibited shuttling behavior of LiPSs in LSBs is schematically shown in Fig. 13, which can be assigned to hollow structure, conductive feature, electrocatalytic activity of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) and its Lewis acid-base interaction with LiPSs.
Dendrite growth inhibition effect of modified separator is estimated by assembling and performing Li-Cu cell. The related results are given in Fig. 14. After running 40 cycles, Celgard cell shows a CE of \(53\%\), whilst the \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cell gives a high CE of \(92.2\%\). After running 100 cycles, Celgard cell shows an extremely low CE of \(6.3\%\). Significantly, the \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cell still exhibits a CE of \(81.4\%\). Notably, Celgard cell shows poor CE stability during cycling. This is due to the weak inhibition effect of Celgard separator on the continuous growth of lithium dendrites, which consumes a large amount of electrolyte as well as \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\), and causes repeated destruction and regeneration of SEI film. After the using of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) separator, the cell possesses enhanced CE stability, suggesting its good dendrite growth inhibition capability. The voltage vs time plots are also given in Fig. 14(a). After 100 cycles of fast \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) deposition/removal, Celgard cell shows incomplete plots, while \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cell gives complete curves. Both at \(1^{\mathrm{st}}\) and \(100^{\mathrm{th}}\) cycles, \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cell shows lower voltage gap than Celgard cell. We also disassembly the cells after different cycles, and investigate the corresponding SEM morphologies of lithium dendrite. After 1 cycle, Celgard cell shows obvious and mossy lithium dendrite. After 100 cycles, more serious dendrite growth is observed. As for \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) cell, a relatively-flat surface is found after 1 cycle, without the formation of lithium dendrite. Even after 100 cycle, a dense and correspondingly-smooth lithium dendrite instead of mossy lithium dendrite, is observed. This indicates that the \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF@C-200}\) separator can effectively inhibit dendrite growth. As mentioned, the over-grown lithium dendrite may puncture the separator, thus causing short circuit of cell. Therefore, the inhibition on dendrite growth is conducive to improve the battery safety. The high specific surface area of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}\) is conducive to reduce the regional electrical density efficiently during lithium plating and thus ensure the uniform lithium growth [69]. Moreover, the abundant N and S heteroatoms in \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}\) are regarded as lithiophilic sites, which reduces the resistance of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transport and further results in the efficient regulation of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) flux through the separator [70]. Overall, \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}/\mathrm{NSCNHF}\) modified separator shows great efficacy in inhibiting polysulfides shuttling as well as lithium dendrites growth, giving rise to safer high-performance LSBs.
## 4 Conclusions
4 ConclusionsIn this contribution, a MOFs-derived hierarchical hybrid of N, S co-doped carbon nanotube hollow architecture confining with \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) nanoparticles \(\mathrm{(CoS_{2}/N S C N H F)}\) is utilized for the modification of separator, which is further used in LSBs. Compared with Celgard separator, the modified separator shows better electrolyte affinity and flame retardancy. By using modified separator, the cell gives an initial discharge capacity of \(1,284.5\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) . After working for 100 cycles, a discharge capacity of \(661.3\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) is delivered. Moreover, the lithium dendrites growth is suppressed, corroborating the higher battery safety. It is believed that this work opens up the opportunity for mining the potentials of MOFs-derived hierarchical hybrid in LSBs.
